{
  "version": 1,
  "language": "spanish",
  "chapter": 319,
  "title": "Review --- Second Looks",
  "drivablePrefix": 5,
  "sourceHash": "fnv1a64:5612a1032b01bf83",
  "lessons": [
    {
      "id": "ES-C319-repaso-la-superficie",
      "sequence": 5060,
      "title": "Review — what the surface does not tell you",
      "headword": "(review)",
      "romanization": "(review)",
      "gloss": "a second pass over five facts that the surface of a word or a page will not give you — an ending, an article, a letter, and a punctuation mark",
      "script": "latin",
      "modality": "voice",
      "derivedModality": "voice",
      "modalityReasons": [
        "no-visual-dependency"
      ],
      "sourceHash": "fnv1a64:dbfcdf40f31b96a2",
      "notice": null,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "type": "warmup",
          "title": "Warm-up",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "What does pronto mean on its own? (Soon — and \"ready\", the sense English kept in prompt.)"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Four things you met separately, gathered because they share a shape: the part you can see is not the part to trust."
            },
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "First. Pronto is about readiness, from promptus; \"soon\" is where readiness ended up."
            },
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Second. Lo siento and perdón both come out as English \"sorry\". The English does not divide them; the Latin does. One names your feeling, the other asks for their pardon."
            },
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Third. El agua looks masculine and is not. The article moved for sound; the object pronoun did not, and says la."
            },
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Fourth. The h on hermano looks like the h on hijo. One stood where a Latin f was breathed aloud for centuries; the other went on the page long after the sound had gone."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "grammar",
          "title": "Grammar Lens: the mark that was designed, not inherited",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "The ¿ belongs on that list from the other side. It is not a survival: the Spanish Royal Academy made it official in 1754, because Spanish leaves the words of a question where they were and something had to warn the reader."
            },
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Four inherited surfaces that mislead, and one deliberate surface that helps."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 1,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 1s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "pronto, then English prompt",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *pronto*, then English *prompt*]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "el agua, then la bebo",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *el agua*, then *la bebo*]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "repeat",
              "times": 2,
              "source": "[REPEAT x2]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "activity",
              "scored": true,
              "id": "ES-C319-repaso-la-superficie-sorry",
              "prompt": "Which of the two sorries asks the other person for something?",
              "assesses": [
                "ES-PRAGMATICS-SORRY-03"
              ],
              "responseSeconds": 10,
              "acceptedResponses": [
                "perdon",
                "perdón",
                "perdon, a pardon is granted"
              ],
              "feedback": {
                "correct": "Perdón asks; lo siento reports.",
                "incorrect": "Perdón — it asks for their pardon."
              }
            },
            {
              "kind": "activity",
              "scored": true,
              "id": "ES-C319-repaso-la-superficie-pronoun",
              "prompt": "El agua takes el. Which pronoun replaces it, and what does that prove?",
              "assesses": [
                "ES-GRAMMAR-PRONOUN-REVEALS-GENDER"
              ],
              "responseSeconds": 14,
              "acceptedResponses": [
                "la, and that agua is feminine",
                "la, agua is feminine underneath",
                "la"
              ],
              "feedback": {
                "correct": "The article bent for sound; the pronoun did not bend at all.",
                "incorrect": "La. The el is a sound rule, so the pronoun is the honest witness."
              }
            },
            {
              "kind": "activity",
              "scored": true,
              "id": "ES-C319-repaso-la-superficie-hache",
              "prompt": "Hijo and hermano both start with a silent h. Which one was once spoken aloud?",
              "assesses": [
                "ES-SOUND-HERMANO-04",
                "ES-HISTORY-RAE-INVERTED-MARKS"
              ],
              "responseSeconds": 14,
              "acceptedResponses": [
                "hijo",
                "hijo, where the h stands for a latin f",
                "hijo and hacer"
              ],
              "feedback": {
                "correct": "Hijo's h was a real breath; hermano's never was — and like the 1754 opening mark, it is on the page rather than in the mouth.",
                "incorrect": "Hijo. Its h replaced a Latin f that was genuinely pronounced; hermano's h is spelling only."
              }
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 4,
          "type": "recall",
          "title": "Wrap-up Recall",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 3,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 3s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Which is the honest witness to a noun's gender? (The object pronoun.) When was ¿ made official? (1754.) Was hermano's h ever a sound? (No.)"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "ES-C319-repaso-salir",
      "sequence": 5070,
      "title": "Review — the five words of a leaving",
      "headword": "(review)",
      "romanization": "(review)",
      "gloss": "the five words of a leaving, said again in order, and the five different places they came from",
      "script": "latin",
      "modality": "voice",
      "derivedModality": "voice",
      "modalityReasons": [
        "no-visual-dependency"
      ],
      "sourceHash": "fnv1a64:4348724ce244d3d2",
      "notice": null,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "type": "warmup",
          "title": "Warm-up",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Two to start. Say \"the street.\" (La calle.) Say \"the corner.\" (La esquina.)"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Say the five in order and hear the distance grow at every step: la calle, la esquina, la huella, el puerto, el faro — the street, its corner, the mark left in the dirt, the water's edge, and the light still visible when the town is not."
            },
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Now the part worth a second visit. Not one of the five was built for this list. Calle was a cattle track, callis. Esquina is a shinbone, Germanic skina. Huella comes from a day spent treading wool, fullāre. Puerto is the Roman harbour, portus, twin to the puerta on land. And faro was an island, Pharos, with a fire on top of a tower."
            },
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Five ordinary nouns out of five unrelated quarries. That is the ratio you should expect from here on."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 1,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 1s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "all five in order — la calle, la esquina, la huella, el puerto, el faro",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: all five in order — *la calle*, *la esquina*, *la huella*, *el puerto*, *el faro*]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "la puerta, then el puerto — the gate on land, the gate off the sea",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *la puerta*, then *el puerto* — the gate on land, the gate off the sea]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "repeat",
              "times": 2,
              "source": "[REPEAT x2]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "activity",
              "scored": true,
              "id": "ES-C319-repaso-salir-order",
              "prompt": "Name the five words of the leaving, in order from the street outwards.",
              "assesses": [
                "ES-LEX-C302-AWAY-01",
                "ES-LEX-C302-AWAY-02",
                "ES-LEX-C302-AWAY-03",
                "ES-LEX-C302-AWAY-04",
                "ES-LEX-C302-AWAY-05"
              ],
              "responseSeconds": 18,
              "acceptedResponses": [
                "la calle, la esquina, la huella, el puerto, el faro",
                "calle, esquina, huella, puerto, faro"
              ],
              "feedback": {
                "correct": "Each one further out than the last.",
                "incorrect": "La calle, la esquina, la huella, el puerto, el faro."
              }
            },
            {
              "kind": "activity",
              "scored": true,
              "id": "ES-C319-repaso-salir-shin",
              "prompt": "Which body part is hiding inside esquina?",
              "assesses": [
                "ES-LEX-C302-AWAY-02"
              ],
              "responseSeconds": 10,
              "acceptedResponses": [
                "the shin",
                "the shinbone",
                "shin, from skina"
              ],
              "feedback": {
                "correct": "A corner is an edge, and so is a shin.",
                "incorrect": "The shin — Germanic skina."
              }
            },
            {
              "kind": "activity",
              "scored": true,
              "id": "ES-C319-repaso-salir-island",
              "prompt": "El faro was a place before it was a thing. Which place?",
              "assesses": [
                "ES-LEX-C302-AWAY-05"
              ],
              "responseSeconds": 12,
              "acceptedResponses": [
                "pharos",
                "pharos, the island off alexandria",
                "the island of pharos"
              ],
              "feedback": {
                "correct": "The name swallowed the object.",
                "incorrect": "Pharos, the island off Alexandria."
              }
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "recall",
          "title": "Wrap-up Recall",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 3,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 3s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "What was fullāre? (Treading cloth in a vat — the trampling that gave huella its footprint.) What does ob portum describe? (A wind standing toward harbour — an opportunity.) And what is a faro in a car park today? (A headlight.)"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "ES-C319-despedida",
      "sequence": 5080,
      "title": "Review — the harbour, the light, and see you soon",
      "headword": "(review)",
      "romanization": "(review)",
      "gloss": "the harbour, the light at its mouth, and the goodbye that says you will be back shortly",
      "script": "latin",
      "modality": "voice",
      "derivedModality": "voice",
      "modalityReasons": [
        "no-visual-dependency"
      ],
      "sourceHash": "fnv1a64:e7910e0b56f46448",
      "notice": null,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "type": "warmup",
          "title": "Warm-up",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "The last two nouns of the run. Say \"the harbour.\" (El puerto.) Say \"the lighthouse.\" (El faro.)"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "A leaving needs a place and a time, and you have both."
            },
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "The place: el puerto, the gate off the sea, and el faro, the light standing at its mouth. The time: hasta pronto — hasta, \"until\", and pronto, \"soon\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Say the whole thing once, slowly, as one leaving:"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "El puerto. El faro. Hasta pronto."
            },
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Three words out of three different centuries and two different languages — a Roman harbour, a Greek island, and an Arabic preposition holding a Latin adjective. You can hear all of that now, which you could not at the start."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 1,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 1s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "el puerto, el faro, hasta pronto",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *el puerto*, *el faro*, *hasta pronto*]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "repeat",
              "times": 2,
              "source": "[REPEAT x2]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "activity",
              "scored": true,
              "id": "ES-C319-despedida-harbour",
              "prompt": "Name the harbour and the lighthouse in Spanish, in that order.",
              "assesses": [
                "ES-LEX-C302-AWAY-04",
                "ES-LEX-C302-AWAY-05"
              ],
              "responseSeconds": 10,
              "acceptedResponses": [
                "el puerto, el faro",
                "el puerto and el faro"
              ],
              "feedback": {
                "correct": "The gate off the sea, and the light at its mouth.",
                "incorrect": "El puerto, el faro."
              }
            },
            {
              "kind": "activity",
              "scored": true,
              "id": "ES-C319-despedida-soon",
              "prompt": "Say see you soon, and give the two words it is made of.",
              "assesses": [
                "ES-LEX-HASTA-PRONTO",
                "ES-LEX-HASTA",
                "ES-LEX-PRONTO"
              ],
              "responseSeconds": 14,
              "acceptedResponses": [
                "hasta pronto: hasta, until, and pronto, soon",
                "hasta pronto, until and soon"
              ],
              "feedback": {
                "correct": "Arabic until, Latin soon.",
                "incorrect": "Hasta pronto — hasta is until, pronto is soon."
              }
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "recall",
          "title": "Wrap-up Recall",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 3,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 3s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Which of the two is the light, and which is the water? (El faro; el puerto.) And what does pronto promise? (Soon — you will be back shortly.)"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "ES-C319-repaso-presentarse",
      "sequence": 5090,
      "title": "Review — an introduction, start to finish",
      "headword": "(review)",
      "romanization": "(review)",
      "gloss": "a whole introduction in five words — both names, the right form of address, and the reply",
      "script": "latin",
      "modality": "voice",
      "derivedModality": "voice",
      "modalityReasons": [
        "no-visual-dependency"
      ],
      "sourceHash": "fnv1a64:e649aeef1836c830",
      "notice": null,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "type": "warmup",
          "title": "Warm-up",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Two to open. Which word is the first name? (El nombre.) And which is the family one? (El apellido.)"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Five words, and between them a complete first meeting."
            },
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "You can name yourself both ways: el nombre and el apellido. You can address the other person: señor or señora, the same Latin senior wearing the -o/-a switch you have used since bueno and buena. And you can answer the introduction: encantado if you are a man, encantada if you are a woman."
            },
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Run it once as one exchange, in your own voice:"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "— Buenos días. Me llamo Ana Ruiz. ¿Cómo se llama usted, señor? — Mi nombre es Luis, y mi apellido es Vargas. Encantado. — Encantada."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 1,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 1s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "el nombre, then el apellido",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *el nombre*, then *el apellido*]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "señor, then señora",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *señor*, then *señora*]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "the whole exchange above, both voices",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: the whole exchange above, both voices]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "repeat",
              "times": 2,
              "source": "[REPEAT x2]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "activity",
              "scored": true,
              "id": "ES-C319-repaso-presentarse-surname",
              "prompt": "Which Spanish word is the family name?",
              "assesses": [
                "ES-LEX-C305-NAME-02"
              ],
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "acceptedResponses": [
                "el apellido",
                "apellido"
              ],
              "feedback": {
                "correct": "The one that came down the family.",
                "incorrect": "El apellido."
              }
            },
            {
              "kind": "activity",
              "scored": true,
              "id": "ES-C319-repaso-presentarse-address",
              "prompt": "Give both forms of address, and the one Latin word behind them.",
              "assesses": [
                "ES-LEX-C305-NAME-03",
                "ES-LEX-C305-NAME-04"
              ],
              "responseSeconds": 14,
              "acceptedResponses": [
                "senor and senora, from senior",
                "señor, señora — senior",
                "senor senora senior"
              ],
              "feedback": {
                "correct": "One word, two endings.",
                "incorrect": "Senor and senora, both from Latin senior."
              }
            },
            {
              "kind": "activity",
              "scored": true,
              "id": "ES-C319-repaso-presentarse-reply",
              "prompt": "A woman is answering an introduction. What does she say?",
              "assesses": [
                "ES-LEX-C305-NAME-05"
              ],
              "responseSeconds": 10,
              "acceptedResponses": [
                "encantada",
                "encantada, with an a"
              ],
              "feedback": {
                "correct": "The ending agrees with the speaker, not the listener.",
                "incorrect": "Encantada — the -a form."
              }
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "recall",
          "title": "Wrap-up Recall",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 3,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 3s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Which Latin word is under señor and señora? (Senior.) Which ending does a woman use to answer an introduction? (-a — encantada.)"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "ES-C319-dos-respuestas",
      "sequence": 5100,
      "title": "Review — two ways to answer an introduction",
      "headword": "(review)",
      "romanization": "(review)",
      "gloss": "the two answers to an introduction — one names the pleasure, one says you have been put under a spell",
      "script": "latin",
      "modality": "voice",
      "derivedModality": "voice",
      "modalityReasons": [
        "no-visual-dependency"
      ],
      "sourceHash": "fnv1a64:78a6d8afb2b4d850",
      "notice": null,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "type": "warmup",
          "title": "Warm-up",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "What does mucho gusto say, word for word? (\"Much pleasure\" — gusto is a taste, and from there a liking.)"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Somebody has just told you their name. Spanish gives you two answers, and they were minted centuries apart."
            },
            {
              "kind": "table",
              "headers": [
                "you say",
                "it literally means"
              ],
              "columns": 2,
              "rowCount": 2,
              "utterances": [
                "you say: mucho gusto. it literally means: much pleasure.",
                "you say: encantado / encantada. it literally means: put under a spell."
              ]
            },
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Mucho gusto is the plain one, and it names a feeling: gusto is Latin gustus, a taste. Encantado is Latin incantāre, to sing a spell onto something — English kept it as enchanted, and the raw noun as incantation."
            },
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Notice what neither of them does: mention the other person. English says \"pleased to meet you\". Spanish names the pleasure, or names the spell, and leaves you out of it. Either answer is right, and mucho gusto never changes its ending while encantado agrees with whoever is speaking."
            }
          ]
        },
