        "waitUntilStopped": [],
        "text": "Before we start: this one needs your eyes, so it is not fully a driving lesson. You will want your eyes, because the lesson points at something written down. You can listen to all of it now and come back to the parts that need looking at once you have stopped."
      },
      "blocks": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "type": "warmup",
          "title": "Warm-up",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Two to open. Which word answers for a thing? (Algo.) And which for a person? (Alguien.)"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Say the five: algo, alguien, nadie, otro, poco."
            },
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Now the part worth a second look. Four of them are built out of two Latin pieces, and once you see the seams the list stops being a list."
            },
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "The ali- pair. Algo is aliquod, \"some other what\". Alguien is aliquem, \"some other whom\". One for things, one for people — a line Latin drew and Spanish kept exactly. And otro is alter, the same neighbourhood: Latin's word for the other of two, which is why English alter, alternate and alter ego all carry a pair inside them."
            },
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "The born ones. Nadie is nati, \"people born\", out of homines nati. Its twin nada was \"a thing born\". One Latin participle, collapsed twice, into two words for absence."
            },
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "And the fifth. Poco is paucus, \"few\" — English paucity straight from the Latin, and English few the same root down the Germanic road after p turned to f. You already carried poco inside tampoco, which is tan plus poco: \"so little.\""
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
