        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Of the five answering words, exactly two change their ending: otro and poco. Both are measuring a thing, so both match the thing."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "otro / otra — another poco / poca — a little."
            },
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Algo, alguien and nadie never move, because none of them is describing a noun. They are the noun."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "culture-pragmatics",
          "title": "Why it's said this way: the same road, a third time",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Poco is Latin paucus, and English few is the same root arriving by the Germanic road, after p turned to f."
            },
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "That shift is not a one-off. Pie and foot. Diente and tooth. Poco and few. Three pairs, one sound law — and once you can hear a Spanish p behind an English f, a large part of English stops looking foreign."
            }
          ]
        },
